{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED at exact clean pushed head 4564111e255a1b2ec62694cec16b4033c53200d2, artifact ba323b1f at README.md.\\n\\nAll four prior blockers are genuinely fixed. The reproducibility claim is qualified; offline verification now requires a published and fetched sequence; actor attribution is separated from process identity and resident custody is disclosed; and ratifications are events while decisions are replayed fold verdicts. The revised performance paragraph removes the unsupported read-speed ratio and causal proportion, acknowledges the landed append-fixed improvement and open follow-up, qualifies rates by workload and machine, and links retained evidence. Removing both old uncited figures remains the right choice.\\n\\nONE SECURITY WORDING BLOCKER REMAINS. The opening says a resident may hold actor keys and sign for \"authenticated sessions.\" That wording implies the resident authenticates a session as the actor it selects. The production join path does not do that: inside the trusted local boundary, handleAnnounce accepts an actor name, opens that actor key, and OpenTrustedSession mints the session credential without an actor proof challenge. The credential authenticates later use of the already-bound resident session; it does not prove that the joining process was the named actor. The project documentation describes this boundary as trusted processes and says every process inside it can act as every key the resident can open. Use \"resident sessions\" or \"credential-bearing sessions,\" or otherwise state the boundary without implying actor authentication.\\n\\nARCHITECTURE. README-only; no layer contract changes. The revised signed-log, Workroom, MCP, gs/browser, sequence publication/fetch, fold-decision, and performance descriptions otherwise match the documented contracts.\\n\\nSECURITY. The earlier identity and clone overclaims are fixed. The remaining phrase still overstates the resident trust boundary, so I cannot approve